Java Practice Five,Processing Annotations with Reflection,"Write a program that uses reflection to find all methods in a class that are marked with your `@MyTest` annotation and invoke them."
Java Practice Five,Reflection - Modifying `private` Fields,"Use reflection to access and modify the value of a `private` field in an object."
Java Practice Five,Creating a Dynamic Proxy,"Use `java.lang.reflect.Proxy` to create a dynamic proxy for an interface that logs method calls."
Java Practice Five,NIO.2 - Walking a File Tree,"Use `Files.walkFileTree()` to recursively visit all files and directories starting from a given path."
Java Practice Five,NIO.2 - Watching a Directory,"Use the `WatchService` API to monitor a directory for file creation, modification, and deletion events."
Java Practice Five,NIO.2 - Memory-Mapped Files,"Use a `MappedByteBuffer` to read a large file, demonstrating memory-mapped I/O."
Java Practice Five,Base64 Encoding/Decoding,"Use `java.util.Base64` to encode a string into Base64 and then decode it back to the original string."
Java Practice Five,HTTP Client (GET Request),"Use `java.net.http.HttpClient` (Java 11+) to send a synchronous GET request to a public API and print the response body."
Java Practice Five,HTTP Client (POST Request),"Use the `HttpClient` to send a POST request with a JSON body to an endpoint."
Java Practice Five,Weak References,"Create a `WeakReference` to an object. Suggest garbage collection and show that the reference becomes null."
Java Practice Five,Phantom References,"Create a `PhantomReference` and a `ReferenceQueue`. Explain in a comment how they are used for pre-mortem cleanup actions."
Java Practice Five,The `StackWalker` API,"Use the `StackWalker` API (Java 9+) to efficiently traverse the current thread's call stack and get information about it."
Java Practice Five,Sealed Interfaces,"Create a `sealed` interface that `permits` only a specific set of classes to implement it."
Java Practice Five,Switch Expressions,"Use a `switch` expression (Java 14+) to return a value, simplifying a traditional `switch` statement."
Java Practice Five,Java Platform Module System (JPMS) - `module-info.java`,"Write a basic `module-info.java` file that `requires` another module and `exports` a package."
Java Practice Five,Creating a Custom `Collector`,"Implement the `Collector` interface to create a custom stream collector that concatenates strings."
Java Practice Five,`StampedLock`,"Use a `StampedLock` to perform an optimistic read, and show how it can be upgraded to a pessimistic read lock if needed."
Java Practice Five,`ThreadLocal` Variables,"Use a `ThreadLocal` variable to give each thread its own instance of a `SimpleDateFormat` object to avoid thread-safety issues."
Java Practice Five,`AtomicInteger`,"Use `AtomicInteger` to safely increment a counter from multiple threads without using `synchronized` blocks."
Java Practice Five,Micro-benchmarking with JMH (Conceptual),"Write a comment explaining what the Java Microbenchmark Harness (JMH) is and what a basic benchmark method looks like."
Java Practice Five,Method Handles,"Use a `MethodHandle` to dynamically find and invoke a method on an object."
Java Practice Five,`Optional.stream()`,"Use `Optional.stream()` (Java 9+) to convert an `Optional` into a stream of either zero or one element."
Java Practice Five,`InputStream.transferTo()`,"Use `transferTo()` (Java 9+) to efficiently copy data from an `InputStream` to an `OutputStream`."
Java Practice Five,`HttpClient` (Asynchronous Request),"Send an asynchronous GET request using the `HttpClient` and use a `CompletableFuture` to process the response."
Java Practice Five,Foreign Function & Memory API (Conceptual),"In a comment, explain the purpose of the Foreign Function & Memory API (Project Panama)."
Java Practice Five,Virtual Threads (Conceptual),"Write a comment explaining the concept of Virtual Threads (Project Loom) and their benefits."
Java Practice Five,Record Serialization,"Create a `record` that implements `Serializable` and demonstrate that it can be written to and read from an `ObjectOutputStream`."
Java Practice Five,Stream `mapMulti()`,"Use `mapMulti()` (Java 16+) as an alternative to `flatMap` for a one-to-many transformation of stream elements."
HashMap,Concept of Key-Value Pairs,"In a comment, explain in your own words what a key-value pair is. Provide a real-world example, like a dictionary."
HashMap,Creating a HashMap,"Write the code to import `java.util.HashMap` and declare a new, empty HashMap that stores student IDs (Integer) as keys and student names (String) as values."
HashMap,Understanding Generics,"In your HashMap declaration `HashMap<Integer, String>`, identify what the `<Integer>` and `<String>` parts represent."
HashMap,Adding a Key-Value Pair (`put`),"Create a HashMap and use the `put()` method to add three entries: (101, ""Alice""), (102, ""Bob""), and (103, ""Charlie"")."
HashMap,Retrieving a Value (`get`),"From the HashMap you created, use the `get()` method to retrieve and print the name associated with the key 102."
HashMap,What Happens with Duplicate Keys,"Add a new entry to your HashMap with a key that already exists (e.g. 101) but a new value (e.g. ""Alicia""). Print the value for key 101 to see what happens."
HashMap,Getting the Size (`size`),"After adding several entries to your HashMap, use the `size()` method to print the total number of key-value pairs it contains."
HashMap,Checking if a Key Exists (`containsKey`),"Write an `if` statement that uses `containsKey()` to check if the key 103 is in your HashMap and prints a confirmation message."
HashMap,Checking if a Value Exists (`containsValue`),"Write code to check if your HashMap contains the value ""Bob"" and print the boolean result."
HashMap,Removing a Key-Value Pair (`remove`),"Use the `remove()` method to delete the entry with the key 102 from your HashMap. Verify by checking its size or the key."
HashMap,Getting a Value That Doesn't Exist,"Try to `get()` a value for a key that is not in the HashMap (e.g. 999). Print the result to see what `get()` returns."
HashMap,Checking if Empty (`isEmpty`),"Create a new, empty HashMap and use the `isEmpty()` method to print whether it is empty. Then add an element and check again."
HashMap,Clearing the HashMap (`clear`),"Add several elements to a HashMap, print its size, then use the `clear()` method to remove all entries. Print the size again."
HashMap,Different Data Types,"Create a HashMap that maps product names (String) to their prices (Double)."
HashMap,Using `keySet` to Get All Keys,"Use the `keySet()` method to get a Set of all the keys in your HashMap. Print the entire set."
HashMap,Iterating Over Keys,"Use a `for-each` loop with `keySet()` to iterate through and print each key in your HashMap on a new line."
HashMap,Using `values` to Get All Values,"Use the `values()` method to get a Collection of all the values in your HashMap. Print the collection."
HashMap,Iterating Over Values,"Use a `for-each` loop with `values()` to iterate through and print each value in your HashMap."
HashMap,Using `entrySet`,"Use the `entrySet()` method to get a Set of all the key-value pairs (entries). Print the entire set."
HashMap,Iterating Over Entries,"Use a `for-each` loop with `entrySet()` to iterate through the HashMap and print each entry in the format ""Key: [key], Value: [value]""."
HashMap,The `getOrDefault` Method,"Use the `getOrDefault()` method to get the value for an existing key. Then, use it to get a value for a non-existent key, providing a default value like ""Not Found""."
HashMap,The `putIfAbsent` Method,"Use `putIfAbsent()` to try and add an entry with a key that already exists. Then, use it to add an entry with a new key. Observe the difference."
HashMap,Creating a Word Counter,"Create a HashMap to count word frequencies. The keys will be words (String) and values will be their counts (Integer)."
HashMap,Word Counter Logic (First Word),"For your word counter, write the logic to handle the first time a word is seen (i.e., it's not yet a key in the map)."
HashMap,Word Counter Logic (Subsequent Words),"For your word counter, write the logic to handle a word that has already been seen (i.e., it's already a key, so you need to increment its value)."
HashMap,A Simple Phone Book,"Create a HashMap to act as a phone book, mapping names (String) to phone numbers (String)."
HashMap,Looking Up a Phone Number,"Write code to look up and print a person's phone number from your phone book map."
HashMap,Updating a Phone Number,"Update an existing person's phone number in your phone book map."
HashMap,Storing User Objects,"Create a simple `User` class with an ID and name. Create a HashMap that maps the user's ID (Integer) to the `User` object itself."
HashMap,Retrieving a User Object,"Retrieve a `User` object from your map and call a method on it (e.g. `user.getName()`)."
HashMap,Null Values,"Demonstrate that a HashMap can store a `null` value by `put()`-ing an entry where the value is `null`."
HashMap,Null Keys,"Demonstrate that a HashMap can have one (and only one) `null` key."
HashMap,HashMap of Lists,"Create a HashMap where the key is a category (String) and the value is a `List<String>` of items in that category (e.g. ""Fruits"" -> [""Apple"", ""Banana""])."
HashMap,Adding to a List in a HashMap,"Write the code to add a new item to the list of an existing category in your HashMap of lists."
HashMap,Creating a New List in a HashMap,"Write the logic to handle adding an item for a brand new category (i.e., you have to create the list first)."
HashMap,A Simple Cache,"Use a HashMap to simulate a simple cache. Before doing a slow calculation, check if the result is already in the map."
HashMap,Cache Logic (Cache Hit),"Write the code for a ""cache hit"" - where the result is found in the map and returned immediately."
HashMap,Cache Logic (Cache Miss),"Write the code for a ""cache miss"" - where the result is not in the map, so you must calculate it and then `put()` it in the map for next time."
HashMap,Replacing a Value (`replace`),"Use the `replace()` method to change the value for a key only if that key already exists in the map."
HashMap,Conditional Replace,"Use the overloaded `replace()` method that takes three arguments to replace a value only if the key is currently mapped to a specific old value."
HashMap,Cloning a HashMap,"Create a HashMap, add data to it, and then create a shallow copy of it using the `clone()` method or the copy constructor."
HashMap,Merging Two HashMaps,"Create two HashMaps and use the `putAll()` method to merge all the entries from the second map into the first."
HashMap,Order of Elements,"Add several elements to a HashMap and print it multiple times. In a comment, note that the order is not guaranteed to be the same as the insertion order."
HashMap,`LinkedHashMap` for Order,"Repeat the previous exercise using a `LinkedHashMap` instead. Note that the insertion order is preserved."
HashMap,Character Frequency Counter,"Write a program that counts the frequency of each character in a string using a `HashMap<Character, Integer>`."
HashMap,Finding the Most Frequent Character,"After building your character frequency map, write code to loop through it and find the character with the highest count."
HashMap,Permissions Check,"Use a `HashMap<String, Boolean>` to store user permissions (e.g. ""canEdit"" -> true, ""canDelete"" -> false)."
HashMap,Checking a Permission,"Write a function that takes a permission name (String) and returns the boolean value from your permissions map."
HashMap,Inventory Management,"Create a `HashMap<String, Integer>` to represent an inventory of products, mapping product names to their stock quantity."
HashMap,Updating Inventory,"Write code to decrease the stock of a product in your inventory map after a sale."
HashMap,Final Project - Gradebook,"Create a gradebook using a `HashMap<String, Integer>`. Write a small program that allows a user to add a student, remove a student, and view a student's grade."
HashMap,Final Project - Configuration,"Use a `HashMap<String, String>` to store application settings (e.g. ""username"" -> ""admin"", ""theme"" -> ""dark""). Write code to load and retrieve a setting."

